            <ArticleTitle>Assessing children's and adolescents' pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>24-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Problems related to the complex task of pain assessment in children and adolescents are identified. Solutions to the problems are presented by describing a variety of tools currently available to assess three distinct dimensions of pain: location, intensity, and quality. Characteristics and strengths of a variety of tools are discussed and examples of each are included. Presented are body outlines to report location, and a variety of tools appropriate for measuring intensity from young preschoolers to adolescents. Worldwide current work describing the development of word lists to describe childhood pain quality is summarized.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
